Sure, here's a step-by-step guide to modify the existing R code to identify all composite numbers that lie between two consecutive triangular numbers excluding the first and the last triangular numbers:
1. First, we need to define a function to check whether a number is composite or not. A composite number is any positive integer greater than 1 that has more than two divisors. We can use the following function to check whether a number is composite or not:
```R
is_composite <- function(n) {
  if (n <= 1) {
    return(FALSE)
  }
  for (i in 2:(sqrt(n))) {
    if (n %% i == 0) {
      return(TRUE)
    }
  }
  return(FALSE)
}
```
This function takes a positive integer `n` as input and returns `TRUE` if `n` is composite, and `FALSE` otherwise. It works by checking whether `n` has any divisors other than 1 and itself.
2. Next, we need to modify the existing code to generate a list of all composite numbers between two consecutive triangular numbers excluding the first and the last triangular numbers. We can do this by modifying the `for` loop in the existing code to iterate over all triangular numbers between `n` and `n+1`, and then checking whether each number between these two triangular numbers is composite or not using the `is_composite` function. If a number is composite, we add it to a list of composite numbers. Here's the modified code:
```R
# Define a function to generate the nth triangular number
triangular <- function(n) {
  return(sum(1:n))
}
# Define a function to check whether a number is composite
is_composite <- function(n) {
  if (n <= 1) {
    return(FALSE)
  }
  for (i in 2:(sqrt(n))) {
    if (n %% i == 0) {
      return(TRUE)
    }
  }
  return(FALSE)
}
# Generate the list of composite numbers between two consecutive triangular numbers
start <- 10
end <- 20
composite_numbers <- c()
for (i in start:end) {
  triangular_i <- triangular(i)
  triangular_iplus1 <- triangular(i+1)
  for (j in (triangular_i+1):(triangular_iplus1-1)) {
    if (is_composite(j)) {
      composite_numbers <- c(composite_numbers, j)
    }
  }
}
```
This code generates the list of composite numbers between the 10th and 20th triangular numbers (excluding the first and last triangular numbers) and stores it in the `composite_numbers` variable.
