    }
}
```
In this example, the Vehicle class serves as the base class, while the Car and Truck classes inherit from it. The Car and Truck classes add their own unique properties and methods, such as NumDoors and PayloadCapacity.
By using inheritance, we can avoid duplicating code for common properties and methods, and instead define them in the base class. We can also use polymorphism to treat instances of the Car and Truck classes as instances of the Vehicle class, allowing us to write code that works with any type of vehicle.
Overall, object-oriented programming and inheritance can be powerful tools for organizing and structuring code in C#, making it more reusable and maintainable.

