Certainly! Here's a sample code for generating a crossword puzzle grid and filling it with words using Go:
```go
package main
import (
    "fmt"
)
type Direction int
const (
    Across Direction = iota
    Down
)
type Word struct {
    Direction Direction
    X, Y      int
    Text      string
}
func main() {
    // Define the size of the grid
    rows, cols := 10, 10
    // Create the grid
    grid := make([][]rune, rows)
    for i := range grid {
        grid[i] = make([]rune, cols)
        for j := range grid[i] {
            grid[i][j] = '.'
        }
    }
    // Define the words to be filled in the grid
    words := []Word{
        {Across, 0, 0, "GO"},
        {Down, 0, 0, "GOLANG"},
        {Across, 0, 2, "CODE"},
        {Down, 2, 0, "VARIABLE"},
        {Across, 2, 3, "FUNC"},
        {Down, 3, 2, "STRUCT"},
    }
    // Fill the grid with words
    for _, word := range words {
        for i, r := range word.Text {
            x, y := word.X, word.Y
            if word.Direction == Across {
                y += i
            } else {
                x += i
            }
            grid[x][y] = r
        }
    }
    // Print the grid
    for i := range grid {
        fmt.Println(string(grid[i]))
    }
}
```
This code defines a `Word` struct that represents a word to be filled in the crossword puzzle, with fields for the direction, starting position, and text of the word. It also defines a `Direction` enum to represent the direction of a word (either across or down).
The main function creates a grid of the specified size, fills it with dots, and defines the words to be filled in the grid. It then loops over each word, filling in the corresponding cells in the grid with the letters of the word.
Finally, the code prints out the completed grid. You can modify the code to suit your needs, such as changing the size of the grid or the words to be filled in.

